  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Several rounds of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms Wednesday through Thursday. An isolated strong \nstorm is possible Thursday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSeveral waves of upper level energy embedded within a larger\nscale trough are expected to pass through our area today-Friday,\nthe most potent of which will be on Thursday. This should lead\nto the development of showers and thunderstorms each day, the\nstrongest and most widespread of which will be on Thursday.\nShort instability profiles today should limit lightning\npotential but deeper instability Thursday should favor more\nlightning. Some modest instability of 500-1000 J/kg, 0-6 km\nshear of up to 30 kts, and steep low-level lapse rates means a\nstrong storm or two cannot be ruled out, especially if storms\nform clusters. The main threat in any strong storm would be\ngusty winds. The scattered nature of these storms means the \narea is not likely to see a widespread wetting rainfall that \ncould help with the drought.\n\nBy 00z Friday, the initial cold front will be moving offshore \nwith upr trof beginning to move east into Canada and precip \nwinding down thru midnight. Outside of isolated showers for \nFriday afternoon with the H5 trof crossing the area the rain \nwill have come to an end. \n\nHigh pressure builds in for the weekend and will continue to worsen\nthe ongoing dry/drought conditions. D0-D1 conditions are present\nin southern and eastern areas of the CWA. Much of the streamflows\nacross the CWA are Below to Much Below Normal for this time of \nyear along with Groundwater wells rapidly falling to below \nnormal to low end normal conditions. Lack of significant widespread\nrainfall will result in worsening conditions in terms of the drought.",
